() Tj
T*
(Abstract) Tj
T*
(Though breastfeeding benefits are well-established, real-world support systems often fail) Tj
T*
(to sustain lactation, particularly in economically disadvantaged populations. We) Tj
T*
(implemented a coordinated, evidence-based breastfeeding-support initiative across 21) Tj
T*
(healthcare municipalities and evaluated its impact on lactation persistence and) Tj
T*
(socioeconomic-related disparities. Health visitors and families in intervention clusters) Tj
T*
(\(n=11\) received comprehensive training, standardized evidence-based protocols, family) Tj
T*
(resource toolkits, and structured follow-up contacts; control clusters \(n=10\) continued) Tj
T*
(customary support practices. Over 600 families and their health visitors completed) Tj
T*
(structured assessments; implementation fidelity and quality were audited. In intervention) Tj
T*
(areas, significantly more families maintained exclusive breastfeeding through six months) Tj
T*
(postpartum. Importantly, socioeconomic-related disparities in breastfeeding cessation were) Tj
T*
(substantially narrowed in intervention clusters while persisting in control regions.) Tj
T*
(Health visitors in intervention clusters reported improved professional confidence and) Tj
T*
(perceived the structured approach as fitting well within existing workflow. Qualitative) Tj
T*
(interviews revealed minimal concerns about stigma despite intensive support targeting.) Tj
T*
(These findings demonstrate that system-level professional breastfeeding support delivered) Tj
T*
(through existing primary-care infrastructure can sustain lactation persistence and narrow) Tj
T*
(equity gaps.) Tj
